{"name":"Solar Flare","hex":"#e67c41","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"solar flare","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent explosion in the Sun's atmosphere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/solar_flare"}],"entries":[{"title":"Solar flare","blocks":[{"text":"A solar flare is a relatively intense, localized emission of electromagnetic radiation in the Sun's atmosphere. Flares occur in active regions and are often, but not always, accompanied by coronal mass ejections, solar particle events, and other eruptive solar phenomena. The occurrence of solar flares varies with the 11-year solar cycle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/X_Class_Solar_Flare_Sends_%E2%80%98Shockwaves%E2%80%99_on_The_Sun_%286819094556%29.jpg/330px-X_Class_Solar_Flare_Sends_%E2%80%98Shockwaves%E2%80%99_on_The_Sun_%286819094556%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_flare"},{"title":"Flare","blocks":[{"text":"A flare is a type of pyrotechnic that produces a bright light or intense heat without an explosion. Flares are used for distress signaling, illumination, or defensive countermeasures in civilian and military applications. Flares may be ground pyrotechnics, projectile pyrotechnics, or parachute-suspended to provide maximum illumination time over a large area. Projectile pyrotechnics may be dropped from aircraft, fired from rocket or artillery, or deployed by flare guns or handheld percussive tubes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/US_Army_52253_Best_Warrior_At_Night.jpg/330px-US_Army_52253_Best_Warrior_At_Night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flare"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Heliospheric-current-sheet.gif/500px-Heliospheric-current-sheet.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Heliospheric-current-sheet.gif/500px-Heliospheric-current-sheet.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeliospheric-current-sheet.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg/500px-X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg/1280px-X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg/500px-X3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An X3.2-class solar flare observed in different wavelengths. Clockwise from top left: 304, 335, 131, and 193 Å","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AX3.2_Solar_flare_on_2013-05-14_at_four_wavelengths.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Coronal_arcade.png/500px-Coronal_arcade.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Coronal_arcade.png/500px-Coronal_arcade.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A post-eruption arcade present after an X5.7-class solar flare during the Bastille Day solar storm[18]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoronal_arcade.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/FlareFrequencies.png/500px-FlareFrequencies.png?utm_source=en.wikip